        treatment: "Severe aortic stenosis is the classical high-risk lesion for non-cardiac surgery because the fixed output cannot compensate for vasodilatation — maintain preload, avoid tachycardia and treat hypotension with a vasoconstrictor rather than fluid alone. Prosthetic valves require careful anticoagulation bridging decisions around surgery. Antibiotic prophylaxis for infective endocarditis is no longer routinely recommended in the United Kingdom for dental or gastrointestinal procedures, though it is considered for those at highest risk.",
